{
  "an10.21:0.1": "Numbered Discourses 10.21 ",
  "an10.21:0.2": "3. The Great Chapter ",
  "an10.21:0.3": "The Lion’s Roar ",
  "an10.21:1.1": "“Mendicants, toward evening the lion, king of beasts, emerges from his den, ",
  "an10.21:1.2": "yawns, ",
  "an10.21:1.3": "surveys the four quarters, ",
  "an10.21:1.4": "and roars his lion’s roar three times. ",
  "an10.21:1.5": "Then he sets out on the hunt. ",
  "an10.21:1.6": "Why is that? ",
  "an10.21:1.7": "‘May I not injure any little creatures on unclear ground.’ ",
  "an10.21:2.1": "‘Lion’ is a term for the Realized One, the perfected one, the fully awakened Buddha. ",
  "an10.21:2.2": "When the Realized One teaches Dhamma to an assembly, this is his lion’s roar. ",
  "an10.21:3.1": "The Realized One possesses ten powers of a Realized One. With these he claims the bull’s place, roars his lion’s roar in the assemblies, and turns the divine wheel. ",
  "an10.21:3.2": "What ten? ",
  "an10.21:3.3": "Firstly, the Realized One truly understands the possible as possible and the impossible as impossible. ",
  "an10.21:3.4": "Since he truly understands this, this is a power of the Realized One. Relying on this he claims the bull’s place, roars his lion’s roar in the assemblies, and turns the divine wheel. ",
  "an10.21:4.1": "Furthermore, the Realized One truly understands the result of deeds undertaken in the past, future, and present in terms of grounds and causes. ",
  "an10.21:4.2": "Since he truly understands this, this is a power of t